</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="inventory"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Inventory before you rearrange</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Write down what is on the desk and what is connected to it. Include the computer, displays, dock, charger, keyboard, mouse, camera, speakers or headset, lamps and power boards. Note which items are used every day and which are occasional.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Then follow one ordinary workflow, such as opening documents, joining a call and switching between two applications. Observe where the setup interrupts you. This gives you a more useful starting point than a shopping list.</p><ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li>Record the connector used by each important device.</li><li>Mark cables that are loose, strained, damaged or across a walking route.</li><li>Note whether the computer or dock has enough ventilation.</li><li>Keep essential work saved before changing connections.</li></ul>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="placement"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Arrange the desk for ordinary use</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Put the main screen in front of you and roughly an arm's length away, then adjust it for comfortable viewing. Reduce glare from windows and lamps. Keep the keyboard and mouse where you can use them without repeatedly reaching across the desk.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before buying a monitor arm, record the screen's weight and size, VESA pattern if it has one, the desk thickness and edge shape, and whether the proposed clamp or grommet method is suitable. Check the monitor-arm manufacturer's load limit and mounting instructions; a compatible connector alone does not prove that the desk or arm can safely carry the screen. Include clearance for cables and the desk's usable depth in the decision.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Change position during the day rather than treating one posture as a permanent solution. If discomfort persists, seek qualified advice rather than assuming a desk adjustment will solve it.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep changes modest") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not lift heavy equipment alone, drill into walls, work on mains wiring or use a damaged power cord. Stop and arrange qualified help when the task is outside a safe equipment adjustment.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="test"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Run a simple everyday check</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">With your normal applications open, test the actions that matter: charging, displaying work, typing, pointing, hearing and being seen. Move around the desk as you would during a workday and check that cables stay clear.</p><div class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto" role="region" aria-label="Home Office Setup reference" tabindex="0"><table role="table" class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm md:text-base"><thead><tr><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Check</th><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Useful observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Check" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Power</td><td data-label="Useful observation" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">The charger reaches the computer without tension and the power board is not overloaded.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Check" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Workspace</td><td data-label="Useful observation" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">The screen is readable, the mouse path is clear and frequently used items are reachable.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Check" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Calls</td><td data-label="Useful observation" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">The camera view, sound and lighting are usable from the normal seated position.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Make one change at a time and keep the original connection available until the new arrangement works.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Source: <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.safeworkaustralia.gov.au/sites/default/files/2023-07/workstation-set-up_infographic_july2023.pdf">Safe Work Australia workstation set-up guidance</a>.</p>
